# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 2
A(('h1', S2))
A(('p', '2.1 The scene in Matthew. Matthew 24 begins with the temple: “And Jesus went out, and departed from the temple: and his disciples came to him for to shew him the buildings of the temple” (24:1). Jesus said, “There shall not be left here one stone upon another, that shall not be thrown down” (24:2). Then “as he sat upon the mount of Olives, the disciples came unto him privately, saying, Tell us, when shall these things be?” (24:3). The chapter that follows is a long answer, read in its own place. The servant comes at its close, after two sayings of Jesus: “Watch therefore: for ye know not what hour your Lord doth come” (24:42), and “Therefore be ye also ready: for in such an hour as ye think not the Son of man cometh” (24:44). The servant begins with the words “Who then” (24:45).'))
A(('p', '2.2 The scene in Luke. Luke’s servant stands in another place. In Luke 12 a multitude has gathered, “an innumerable multitude of people,” and Jesus “began to say unto his disciples first of all, Beware ye of the leaven of the Pharisees, which is hypocrisy” (12:1). He then speaks to “his disciples” about life and food and clothing (12:22) and says, “Fear not, little flock; for it is your Father’s good pleasure to give you the kingdom” (12:32). In 12:35–40 He speaks of servants who wait for their lord and of a goodman of the house and a thief, and He closes with the words, “Be ye therefore ready also: for the Son of man cometh at an hour when ye think not” (12:40). The verse that follows is Peter’s question (12:41).'))
A(('call', 'Peter’s Question Comes Before the Servant in Luke',
   '“Then Peter said unto him, Lord, speakest thou this parable unto us, or even to all?” (Luke 12:41). Luke says “this parable” and does not say which of the sayings before it Peter means. The Lord’s reply is another question, “Who then is that faithful and wise steward” (12:42). Luke does not say whether the reply answers “us” or “all,” and nothing is added here to decide it.'))
A(('p', '2.3 The words that join the servant to what comes before. In both accounts the servant opens with “Who then.” In Matthew the word follows “Therefore be ye also ready” (24:44). In Luke it follows Peter’s question (12:41). The two accounts do not open the same way, and neither opening is read into the other.'))
A(('p', '2.4 The prophetic context. Jesus’ ministry in the Gospels is a ministry in which He says He was sent to preach the kingdom. In Luke He says, “I must preach the kingdom of God to other cities also: for therefore am I sent” (4:43). In Matthew He preached, “Repent: for the kingdom of heaven is at hand” (4:17). The word kingdom stands in the setting of each account, in the disciples’ question of Matthew 24:3 about a sign and in Luke 12:32 where Jesus speaks of the kingdom the Father is pleased to give. The servant is read as spoken in that ministry and not as a saying that floats free of it.'))
A(('p', '2.5 Why Jesus tells it here. Matthew gives one occasion: the disciples’ private question on the mount of Olives, and Jesus’ call to watch and to be ready. Luke gives another: the sayings to His disciples in a crowded place and Peter’s question. The two occasions are not the same, and the study of this story keeps each where Matthew and Luke place it.'))
A(('tbl', 'The verses that set the scenes are laid out below.', [
    ['Matt 24:3', '“Tell us, when shall these things be?”', 'The disciples’ question, asked privately.'],
    ['Matt 24:42', '“Watch therefore: for ye know not what hour your Lord doth come”', 'A saying of Jesus before the servant.'],
    ['Matt 24:44', '“Therefore be ye also ready”', 'The saying just before “Who then.”'],
    ['Luke 12:1', '“he began to say unto his disciples first of all”', 'Whom Jesus speaks to at the start.'],
    ['Luke 12:41', '“Lord, speakest thou this parable unto us, or even to all?”', 'Peter’s question, put before the servant.'],
    ['Luke 4:43', '“I must preach the kingdom of God to other cities also”', 'What Jesus says He was sent to do.'],
], [0.15, 0.45, 0.40]))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 3
A(('h1', S3))
A(('p', 'The two accounts are read here in the order Jesus gives them, one at a time. Nothing is rearranged, nothing is added from another parable, and no meaning is given to any part before the text gives it.'))
A(('p', '3.1 The question in Matthew (24:45). “Who then is a faithful and wise servant, whom his lord hath made ruler over his household, to give them meat in due season?” The story opens as a question. It names a servant, a lord, a household and a task, to give them “meat in due season.” The question asks who the servant is and does not answer itself in the same verse.'))
A(('p', '3.2 The blessing and the promotion in Matthew (24:46–47). “Blessed is that servant, whom his lord when he cometh shall find so doing. Verily I say unto you, That he shall make him ruler over all his goods.” The sequence has three steps. The lord comes. He finds the servant “so doing.” He makes him ruler over all his goods. The text calls the servant blessed and states what the lord will do.'))
A(('p', '3.3 The other servant in Matthew (24:48–51). “But and if that evil servant shall say in his heart, My lord delayeth his coming; And shall begin to smite his fellowservants, and to eat and drink with the drunken; The lord of that servant shall come in a day when he looketh not for him, and in an hour that he is not aware of, And shall cut him asunder, and appoint him his portion with the hypocrites: there shall be weeping and gnashing of teeth.” The sequence has six steps. The servant says in his heart that his lord delays. He begins to smite his fellowservants. He eats and drinks with the drunken. The lord comes in a day and hour the servant does not expect. The lord cuts him asunder. The lord appoints him his portion with the hypocrites, and the account closes with weeping and gnashing of teeth.'))
A(('p', '3.4 What Matthew’s story says and does not say. The text says the servant is “faithful and wise,” that the lord “hath made” him ruler over his household, and that the lord will make him ruler “over all his goods.” It calls the other servant “that evil servant” and says what he says “in his heart” and what he does. It does not say who the lord is, where he has gone, how long he is away, who the household or the fellowservants are, what the “meat” is, or what the goods are. It does not say whether the evil servant is the same servant as the faithful one at a later time, and it does not say what becomes of him after the portion is appointed.'))
A(('p', '3.5 The question and the blessing in Luke (12:42–44). “And the Lord said, Who then is that faithful and wise steward, whom his lord shall make ruler over his household, to give them their portion of meat in due season? Blessed is that servant, whom his lord when he cometh shall find so doing. Of a truth I say unto you, that he will make him ruler over all that he hath.” Luke’s first word for the man is “steward,” and then “servant.” The household, the portion of meat in due season, the lord’s coming, the finding “so doing” and the promotion stand in the same order as in Matthew, with different words in places.'))
A(('p', '3.6 The other servant in Luke (12:45–46). “But and if that servant say in his heart, My lord delayeth his coming; and shall begin to beat the menservants and maidens, and to eat and drink, and to be drunken; The lord of that servant will come in a day when he looketh not for him, and at an hour when he is not aware, and will cut him in sunder, and will appoint him his portion with the unbelievers.” Luke says “that servant” and does not call him evil. He beats “the menservants and maidens,” he eats and drinks and is drunken, and the lord “will cut him in sunder” and appoint him his portion “with the unbelievers.” Luke’s account does not close with weeping and gnashing of teeth.'))
A(('p', '3.7 What only Luke gives (12:47–48). “And that servant, which knew his lord’s will, and prepared not himself, neither did according to his will, shall be beaten with many stripes. But he that knew not, and did commit things worthy of stripes, shall be beaten with few stripes. For unto whomsoever much is given, of him shall be much required: and to whom men have committed much, of him they will ask the more.” The verses add a servant who knew his lord’s will and a man who knew not, two measures of stripes, and a closing statement about what is required of those given much. Matthew has nothing that corresponds to them.'))
A(('p', '3.8 The two accounts side by side. Matthew 24:45–51 and Luke 12:42–48 are laid out together below so that neither is read into the other.'))
A(('ctbl', '', [
    ['Point', 'Matthew 24:45–51', 'Luke 12:42–48'],
    ['What comes before', 'The disciples’ question and “Therefore be ye also ready”', 'Peter’s question, “unto us, or even to all?”'],
    ['How it opens', '“Who then is a faithful and wise servant”', '“Who then is that faithful and wise steward”'],
    ['The task', '“to give them meat in due season”', '“to give them their portion of meat in due season”'],
    ['The promotion', '“ruler over all his goods”', '“ruler over all that he hath”'],
    ['The other servant', '“that evil servant”', '“that servant”'],
    ['What he does', '“smite his fellowservants, and to eat and drink with the drunken”', '“beat the menservants and maidens, and to eat and drink, and to be drunken”'],
    ['The cutting', '“cut him asunder”', '“cut him in sunder”'],
    ['His portion', '“with the hypocrites”', '“with the unbelievers”'],
    ['How it closes', '“weeping and gnashing of teeth”', 'Stripes, many and few, and “much required”'],
], [0.19, 0.41, 0.40]))
A(('p', '3.9 What the stories leave unsaid. In neither account does Jesus say who the lord is, who the servant or steward is, or what the household is. Neither says what the “meat in due season” is or how long the lord is away. Each account is read as it stands.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 4
A(('h1', S4))
A(('p', '4.1 What Jesus says beside the story. Jesus gives no explanation of the servant. No sentence after Matthew 24:51 or Luke 12:48 says what the lord, the servant or the household is. The words Jesus speaks beside the story are recorded below as they stand, and none of them names a part of the story.'))
A(('ctbl', '', [
    ['What Jesus says', 'His words', 'Verse'],
    ['Before the servant in Matthew', '“Watch therefore: for ye know not what hour your Lord doth come”', 'Matt 24:42'],
    ['Before the servant in Matthew', '“Therefore be ye also ready: for in such an hour as ye think not the Son of man cometh”', 'Matt 24:44'],
    ['Before the servant in Luke', '“Be ye therefore ready also: for the Son of man cometh at an hour when ye think not”', 'Luke 12:40'],
    ['In answer to Peter', '“Who then is that faithful and wise steward”', 'Luke 12:42'],
    ['Of the faithful servant', '“Verily I say unto you, That he shall make him ruler over all his goods”', 'Matt 24:47'],
    ['Of the faithful servant', '“Of a truth I say unto you, that he will make him ruler over all that he hath”', 'Luke 12:44'],
], [0.26, 0.54, 0.20]))
A(('p', '4.2 What Jesus does not name in Matthew. Jesus does not identify the lord. He does not state in so many words that the lord of the servant is “your Lord” of 24:42 or “the Son of man” of 24:44, although the servant follows those sayings. Jesus does not identify the servant, the household, the “meat in due season,” the goods, the fellowservants, the evil servant, the hypocrites or the weeping and gnashing of teeth.'))
A(('p', '4.3 What Jesus does not name in Luke. Jesus does not identify the lord, the steward, the household or the “portion of meat in due season.” He does not state that the lord of the story is “the Son of man” of 12:40. He does not identify the menservants and maidens, the unbelievers, “that servant, which knew his lord’s will,” or “he that knew not.” He does not say who is meant by “us” or by “all” in Peter’s question, and His reply does not say.'))
A(('call', 'A Warning Against Adding',
   'Jesus names no part of the servant story. A reader who says that the lord is a certain person, that the servant is a certain office or a certain company, that the household or the meat stands for something, or that the evil servant, the hypocrites or the unbelievers are certain persons is supplying what the Lord did not.'))
A(('p', '4.4 The accounts keep their own words. The “hypocrites” of Matthew 24:51 and the “unbelievers” of Luke 12:46 are different words in different accounts, and neither explains the other. The verses about the servant who knew and the man who knew not (Luke 12:47–48) belong to Luke’s account alone, and Matthew’s account does not supply what they leave unnamed.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 5
A(('h1', S5))
A(('p', 'This section states the meaning that arises from the comparison itself. It adds no identification beyond Section 4 and assigns an identity to no part of the story.'))
A(('p', '5.1 The comparison. A lord leaves a servant over his household to give them their food in due season. The meaning of the comparison is in the movement: a task given, a lord who delays and then comes, a servant found at his task or found abusing it, and two outcomes that are far apart.'))
A(('p', '5.2 What the comparison communicates.'))
A(('bul', [
    '**Faithfulness is shown by what the servant is found doing.** The lord “shall find so doing,” and the blessing follows what he finds.',
    '**The faithful servant receives more.** The one made ruler over the household is made ruler “over all his goods.”',
    '**The delay tests the servant.** The other servant says “in his heart” that the lord delays, and he acts on it. The comparison sets his thought before his conduct.',
    '**The coming is not looked for.** The lord comes “in a day when he looketh not for him, and in an hour that he is not aware of.”',
    '**The two outcomes are far apart.** One is blessed and promoted. The other is cut asunder or cut in sunder and given a portion with the hypocrites or the unbelievers.',
    '**In Luke, what is required matches what was given.** “Unto whomsoever much is given, of him shall be much required.”',
]))
A(('p', '5.3 What the hearers were meant to understand. In Matthew the hearers were the disciples who asked privately on the mount of Olives (24:3). In Luke Jesus spoke to His disciples, and Peter put the question (12:1, 41). They were meant to understand that one who has been given a charge is to be found at it when the lord comes, that a delay is no leave to abuse those under his hand, and that the day is not known beforehand. These are comparisons about that ministry and its Kingdom setting, and they are not about the church of the Body.'))
A(('p', '5.4 What this section does not do. It does not say who the lord or the servant is, and it does not say what the household, the meat or the goods are. It does not say who the fellowservants, the hypocrites or the unbelievers are. It does not say where or when any reckoning stands. It does not turn a servant’s faithfulness or failure into a step by which a person is saved or lost. It draws no meaning from another parable into this story.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 6
A(('h1', S6))
A(('p', 'The servant belongs to Matthew 24 and Luke 12. It is kept apart here from four neighbors.'))
A(('tbl', '', [
    ['Matt 25:14–30', '“the kingdom of heaven is as a man travelling into a far country”', 'The Talents. A man gives his servants goods in his absence, with its own setting after the Ten Virgins and its own study. Its servants and its reckoning are not read into this story.'],
    ['Luke 19:11–27', '“A certain nobleman went into a far country”', 'The Minas. Told near Jerusalem to a hearing that thought the kingdom would appear immediately, with its own study.'],
    ['Matt 25:1–13', '“Then shall the kingdom of heaven be likened unto ten virgins”', 'The Ten Virgins. The next story in Matthew, with a bridegroom and lamps, in its own study.'],
    ['Matt 24:43–44; Luke 12:35–40', '“if the goodman of the house had known in what watch the thief would come”', 'The Thief and Watchful Servants. The sayings that come just before the servant in both accounts, with their own study. They are not read into this story.'],
], [0.17, 0.33, 0.50]))
A(('p', '6.1 Differences in setting. Matthew’s servant stands after the disciples’ question on the mount of Olives. Luke’s stands after Peter’s question in a gathering of a multitude. The Talents and the Ten Virgins follow later in Matthew. The Minas is told on another road. Each is read in its own place.'))
A(('p', '6.2 Differences in characters. The servant story has a lord, a servant or steward, a household, fellowservants (in Matthew) or menservants and maidens (in Luke), and a second servant. The Talents has servants given goods, and the Ten Virgins has a bridegroom and virgins. No character is carried from one account to another.'))
A(('p', '6.3 Differences in narrative movement. The servant moves from a question to a blessing, then to a servant who says his lord delays, and to a coming and a cutting. The Talents moves from goods given to a reckoning of what was done with them. The Ten Virgins moves from lamps and oil to a closed door. None of those movements is read into the servant.'))
A(('p', '6.4 Differences in interpretive detail. Three details must not be carried across. First, “that evil servant” of Matthew 24:48 is not the servant of Matthew 25:26 or 25:30, which are other sayings in another story. Second, “ruler over all his goods” (Matthew 24:47) and “ruler over many things” (Matthew 25:21, 23) are different words in different stories. Third, the “weeping and gnashing of teeth” of Matthew 24:51 is not joined to the same words in other places, and it is not found in Luke’s account.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 7
A(('h1', S7))
A(('p', '7.1 The original address. In Matthew, Jesus speaks to the disciples who came to Him privately on the mount of Olives (24:3). In Luke, He speaks to His disciples, and Peter puts a question to Him (12:1, 41). Both belong to the earthly ministry in which He said He was sent to preach the kingdom of God (Luke 4:43) and preached that the kingdom of heaven is at hand (Matthew 4:17).'))
A(('call', 'Standing Statements',
   'Jesus speaks during His earthly Kingdom ministry before the Acts overlap. The Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. This is a principled lesson, not a transfer of Kingdom identity to the Body.'))
A(('p', '7.2 The Acts overlap boundary. Israel’s prophetic program did not end with the Gospels, and it did not stop when Paul was called. The Kingdom message is still preached to Israel in Acts 1–8, and through Acts 9–28 Israel’s program and the Mystery run side by side. The two never merge, transfer or absorb one another. Israel’s program was nationally suspended at Acts 28. The servant was told before any of that, and it is not read as though the church of the Body were already present.'))
A(('p', '7.3 The Mystery begins with Paul. The Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. Nothing in Matthew 24 or Luke 12 speaks of the Body of Christ, and neither passage is read as though the Body were already formed. The Mystery that was hid in God and made known through Paul is read in Paul’s letters.'))
A(('p', '7.4 What is not transferred. The lord, the servant, the household, the coming of the lord, the promotion over all his goods, the cutting asunder and the portion with the hypocrites or the unbelievers are not a description of the Body of Christ or of any office in the church. The story is not read as the Body’s hope of a coming or as a warning that the Body can be cut off. A servant’s faithfulness or failure is an action in a story and is not a mechanism of salvation. The Body’s salvation is read in Paul’s letters and not in this story: “For by grace are ye saved through faith; and that not of yourselves: it is the gift of God: Not of works, lest any man should boast” (Ephesians 2:8–9).'))
A(('p', '7.5 Responsible present learning. A reader today may draw lessons from what the comparison shows. They are lessons of conduct and not of identity.'))
A(('bul', [
    '**A lesson of faithfulness.** The servant is blessed because he is found “so doing.” A reader learns to do the task given whether or not the lord is in view.',
    '**A lesson of care for others.** The evil servant smites his fellowservants. A reader learns not to use a charge to abuse those under it.',
    '**A lesson against presuming on delay.** The servant who says “My lord delayeth his coming” is caught in a day he did not look for. A reader learns not to take delay as leave to neglect.',
    '**A lesson of responsibility.** “Unto whomsoever much is given, of him shall be much required.” A reader learns to weigh what he has been given.',
    '**A lesson of humility about the hour.** The coming is “in an hour that he is not aware of.” A reader learns not to treat an unknown hour as a date to work out.',
]))
A(('p', 'None of these lessons says that the Body is the servant, that the Body is the household, or that the Body will be cut asunder. They are principles drawn from what the story shows.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 8
A(('h1', S8))
A(('p', 'Jesus asks who is a faithful and wise servant whom his lord has made ruler over his household to give them meat in due season. He says that servant is blessed when his lord comes and finds him so doing, and that the lord will make him ruler over all his goods. He tells of another servant who says in his heart that his lord delays, who smites his fellowservants and eats and drinks with the drunken, and whose lord comes in a day and hour he does not expect, cuts him asunder and appoints him his portion with the hypocrites (Matthew 24:45–51). Luke gives the story after Peter’s question, calls the man a steward, says the other servant’s portion is with the unbelievers, and adds the servant who knew his lord’s will, the one who knew not, and the statement that much is required of those given much (12:42–48). Jesus names no part of the story. He spoke during His earthly Kingdom ministry before the Acts overlap, and the Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. Any reading that says who the lord or the servant is, or that makes a servant’s faithfulness or failure a way of being saved or lost, says more than the text says.'))
A(('pk', '**Teaching Summary.**'))
A(('bul', [
    '**The sequence:** a question about a faithful and wise servant, a blessing and a promotion, a servant who says his lord delays and abuses those under him, and a lord who comes unlooked for and cuts him off from the household.',
    '**The meaning:** one given a charge is found at it or found failing when the lord comes, and the outcomes are far apart.',
    '**The boundaries:** Jesus names no part of the story; the lord, the servant, the household, the meat and the portions are not identified.',
    '**The present lesson:** faithfulness, care for others, no presuming on delay, responsibility and humility about the hour, drawn as principles and not as a transfer.',
]))

